1. Eager Initialization Singleton

public class EagerSingleton {
    private static final EagerSingleton instance = new EagerSingleton();

    private EagerSingleton() {}

    public static EagerSingleton getInstance() {
        return instance;
    }
}


2. Lazy Initialization Singleton

public class LazySingleton {
    private static LazySingleton instance;

    private LazySingleton() {}

    public static LazySingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null)
            instance = new LazySingleton();
        return instance;
    }
}


3. Thread-safe Singleton Using synchronized

public class SynchronizedSingleton {
    private static SynchronizedSingleton instance;

    private SynchronizedSingleton() {}

    public static synchronized SynchronizedSingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null)
            instance = new SynchronizedSingleton();
        return instance;
    }
}



4.) Double-Checked Locking Singleton 
public class DoubleCheckedLockingSingleton {
    private static volatile DoubleCheckedLockingSingleton instance;

    private DoubleCheckedLockingSingleton() {}

    public static DoubleCheckedLockingSingleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (DoubleCheckedLockingSingleton.class) {
                if (instance == null)
                    instance = new DoubleCheckedLockingSingleton();
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}




5.) Bill Pugh Singleton (Best Practice – Inner Static Class)
📌 Uses a static inner helper class. JVM loads the inner class only when it's referenced, so it's lazy-loaded and thread-safe.


public class BillPughSingleton {

    private BillPughSingleton() {}

    private static class SingletonHelper {
        private static final BillPughSingleton INSTANCE = new BillPughSingleton();
    }

    public static BillPughSingleton getInstance() {
        return SingletonHelper.INSTANCE;
    }
}

explanation of bill pugh

At first this looks strange.
Why create another class?

private static class SingletonHelper
The trick comes from how JVM class loading works.

When this class is loaded: BillPughSingleton the nested helper class does not need to be initialized immediately.
So initially:

BillPughSingleton loaded
        |
        |
SingletonHelper not initialized
        |
No Singleton object yet


Then someone calls BillPughSingleton.getInstance()  which accesses: SingletonHelper.INSTANCE
Now the JVM initializes:SingletonHelper and runs:
private static final BillPughSingleton INSTANCE =  new BillPughSingleton();
So creation happens only when requested.

Why is Bill Pugh thread-safe?
Because JVM guarantees class initialization is synchronized/thread-safe.
Suppose Thread A and Thread B simultaneously call: getInstance()
and both try to initialize:SingletonHelperThe JVM ensures only one thread initializes the class.
So only one:new BillPughSingleton();
is executed.You get thread safety without manually writing:


